<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="Cairns hike planner" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
  <metadata>
    <name>Teneriffe Falls</name>
    <desc>Mount Teneriffe trailhead to Teneriffe Falls and back. 9.3 km, +521 m, −518 m.</desc>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="47.486520" lon="-121.710207">
    <ele>247.7</ele>
    <name>1 Mount Teneriffe trailhead</name>
    <desc>Discover Pass on the dashboard. Restrooms at the lot, no water: carry 1–1.5 L each. DNR gate: it opens about an hour after dawn and closes about an hour before dusk; the posted times on the sign win. The old bus turnaround is no parking.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.494528" lon="-121.706705">
    <ele>357.6</ele>
    <name>2 Teneriffe road</name>
    <desc>The access trail meets the old Teneriffe road. Follow the signs for Teneriffe Falls: a gentle old roadbed, about 8%, for the next 1.5 km.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.498760" lon="-121.710046">
    <ele>499.6</ele>
    <name>3 Switchbacks start, 500 m</name>
    <desc>The trail leaves the road grade and climbs: rocky switchbacks in and out of a talus field, about 240 m of climb in the last 1.2 km. Eat now. Watch your ankles on the talus.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.500531" lon="-121.710447">
    <ele>590.8</ele>
    <name>4 Connector junction, 590 m</name>
    <desc>The Teneriffe Falls Connector (an unsigned shortcut from the Mount Si side) joins here. Keep climbing: there is a lower viewpoint where many people stop by mistake; the falls are 3–4 switchbacks above it.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.501850" lon="-121.708163">
    <ele>739.7</ele>
    <name>5 Teneriffe Falls</name>
    <desc>The falls drop in tiers down a steep rock gully. Thin after a dry summer, loud after rain. The rocks at the base are slick and drop off steeply: stay back from the edge. The boot path up beside the falls (the Kamikaze route to the summit) is unofficial and steep; DNR asks hikers to stay off it.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.500531" lon="-121.710447">
    <ele>590.8</ele>
    <name>6 Connector junction (down)</name>
    <desc>Keep on the switchbacks down. The connector branching off leads toward the Mount Si trailhead, not back to your car.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.498760" lon="-121.710046">
    <ele>499.6</ele>
    <name>7 Bottom of the switchbacks</name>
    <desc>Off the talus and back on the road grade. Easy walking from here.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.494528" lon="-121.706705">
    <ele>357.6</ele>
    <name>8 Teneriffe road (down)</name>
    <desc>Leave the road here and take the access trail down to the lot (signed to the trailhead). The road carries on up toward Mount Teneriffe.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.486520" lon="-121.710207">
    <ele>247.7</ele>
    <name>9 Mount Teneriffe trailhead</name>
    <desc>Finish. Back at the car, before the gate closes.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>Teneriffe Falls</name>
